{"title": "Deprecating Span Events API", "url": "https://opentelemetry.io/blog/2026/deprecating-span-events/", "publisher": "OpenTelemetry blog", "date": "17 Mar 2026"}, {"title": "OpenTelemetry logging and you", "url": "https://opentelemetry.io/blog/2025/opentelemetry-logging-and-you/", "publisher": "OpenTelemetry blog", "date": "2025"}, {"title": "Logs API specification", "url": "https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/logs/api/", "publisher": "OpenTelemetry"}, {"title": "Tracing API specification", "url": "https://opentelemetry.io/docs/specs/otel/trace/api/", "publisher": "OpenTelemetry"}]}-->
<p>If you have instrumented code with OpenTelemetry, you have probably called <code>Span.AddEvent</code> or <code>Span.RecordException</code>. In March 2026 the project announced it is deprecating that API. New events should be emitted as logs, correlated with the active span through context<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</p>
<p>It sounds like a small API change. For platform teams it touches instrumentation guidelines, dashboards, cost and, increasingly, AI telemetry.</p>

<section class="block">
<h2>Why the change</h2>
<p>OpenTelemetry has offered two ways to emit events correlated with traces: span events via the Tracing API, and log-based events via the Logs API<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. The announcement lists the problems with having both: split guidance for library authors, duplicate concepts for operators, and slower evolution because every improvement had to be specified twice<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. The community has converged on a simpler model: <strong>events are logs with names</strong>, emitted via the Logs API and correlated through context, a direction set out in an earlier post on logging<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What changes and what doesn't</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Changing</th><th>Staying the same</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Span event APIs such as <code>AddEvent</code> and <code>RecordException</code> are deprecated in the tracing specification</td><td>Existing span event data remains valid in the OTLP trace model</td></tr>
<tr><td>SDKs make log-based events first-class</td><td>Correlation across signals works the same way</td></tr>
<tr><td>Instrumentations and semantic conventions move to log-based events in their next major versions</td><td>Compatibility options can still show events on spans</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>As the authors put it, they are deprecating the API for recording span events, not the ability to see events attached to spans<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</p>
<div class="callout"><p>The practical risk isn't broken code. It's a dashboard or alert that quietly stops showing exceptions because they now arrive as logs instead of span events.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What I'd do now</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Update instrumentation guidelines.</strong> New code emits events and exceptions as log-based events correlated with the current span.</li>
<li><strong>Check the logs pipeline.</strong> If logs aren't collected through OpenTelemetry yet, events will have nowhere to go. A Collector gateway, as in <a href="/insights/opentelemetry-collector-deployment-patterns/">Collector deployment patterns</a>, is the natural place to route them.</li>
<li><strong>Audit dashboards and alerts</strong> that depend on span events, especially exception counts and error views, and test them after each instrumentation upgrade, as the project recommends<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Confirm backend support</strong> for showing log-based events in trace views, so engineers don't lose context when debugging.</li>
<li><strong>Watch costs.</strong> Moving events to the logs pipeline may change how they are priced and retained by your vendor. Model it before the switch.</li>
</ol>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Why this matters for AI telemetry</h2>
<p>AI applications produce a lot of event-like data: prompts, responses, tool calls, guardrail decisions. Treating these as named, structured log events correlated with traces fits the GenAI observability work described in <a href="/insights/llm-observability-opentelemetry-genai/">LLM observability with OpenTelemetry</a>. It also makes it easier to apply separate retention, redaction and access rules to sensitive content than when it is embedded in spans.</p>
</section>

